+$|\langle Aw,u\rangle|=|\langle w,v\rangle|\le\|w\|\|v\|$ by [F5],
+so this functional is bounded in the ambient $H$ norm on $D(A)$.
+By [F6], $u\in D(A^*)$ and its unique representing vector is $A^*u=v$. Self-adjointness, including equality of domains, yields $u\in D(A)$ and $Au=v$. Thus $A$ is closed. For $w\in D((I+A)^*)$, write $z=(I+A)^*w$.
+Subtracting the identity term gives
+$\langle Au,w\rangle=\langle u,z-w\rangle$ for every $u\in D(A)$.
+By [F5] this is bounded by $\|u\|\|z-w\|$, so [F6] gives
+$w\in D(A^*)$ and $A^*w=z-w$, or $(I+A)^*w=w+A^*w$. Conversely, if $w\in D(A^*)$, then $\langle(I+A)u,w\rangle=\langle u,w+A^*w\rangle$ for every $u\in D(A)$. The functional in $u$ is bounded by $\|u\|\|w+A^*w\|$ by [F5],
+so [F6] gives $w\in D((I+A)^*)$ with $(I+A)^*w=w+A^*w$. Thus $(I+A)^*=I+A^*=I+A$ with domain $D(A)$, so $I+A$ is self-adjoint. If $u_n\in D(A)$ and $(I+A)u_n$ converges, the lower bound makes $(u_n)$ Cauchy; closedness then puts the limit in the range, so the range of $I+A$ is closed. If $y$ is orthogonal to that range, then
+$\langle(I+A)x,y\rangle=0=\langle x,0\rangle$ for all $x\in D(A)$;
+[F6] with representing vector zero gives $y\in\ker(I+A)^*=\ker(I+A)$, which is zero by the same lower bound; the range is therefore dense and hence all of $H$. Thus $B=(I+A)^{-1}$ exists everywhere, is bounded with norm at most $1$, is injective, and has range $D(A)$, which is dense. [given, A1, A2, F5, F6, algebra]
 
 2.1 If $x=(I+A)u$ and $y=(I+A)v$, then $\langle Bx,y\rangle=\langle u,(I+A)v\rangle=\langle(I+A)u,v\rangle=\langle x,By\rangle$, so $B$ is self-adjoint. Also $\langle Bx,x\rangle=\langle u,(I+A)u\rangle=\|u\|^2+\langle Au,u\rangle\ge0$, so $B$ is positive. For nonreal $z$, Cauchy–Schwarz [F5] and self-adjointness give $\|(B-zI)w\|\ge|\operatorname{Im}z|\,\|w\|$ and the same lower bound for its adjoint, so its range is closed and dense. For real $z<0$, positivity and [F5] give $\|(B-zI)w\|\ge(-z)\|w\|$, again giving closed and dense range. For real $z>1$, the Neumann series $(zI-B)^{-1}=z^{-1}\sum_{n\ge0}(B/z)^n$ converges in operator norm, so $B-zI$ is invertible as well. Hence $\sigma(B)\subseteq[0,1]$. [given, step 1.1, F5, algebra]
 
